Report: Implementing a Naive Bayes Classifier for Spam Email Detection

Objective:
The purpose of this analysis is to build and evaluate a Naive Bayes classifier to distinguish between spam and non-spam emails in a dataset. The model’s effectiveness will be assessed through accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score metrics.

Dataset Overview:
The dataset comprises labeled emails categorized as "spam" or "not spam," with each email containing features such as word frequency, character counts, and possibly specific keyword indicators. For this analysis, key text-processing steps were applied, including tokenization, stop-word removal, and term frequency-inverse document frequency (TF-IDF) transformation, to standardize the input for modeling.

Model Selection:
The Naive Bayes classifier was chosen due to its efficiency and strong performance on text classification tasks. Specifically, the Multinomial Naive Bayes variant is suitable here, as it works well with discrete data, such as word counts or TF-IDF scores from text documents.
